{"kind":"assert","text":"Hugh: qualify the publication-order advice in #19016. Its main recommendation—one concise delivery account and short path companions—still stands. The instruction to publish the intended reporting artifact after all companions is too broad when those companions describe behavior produced by that artifact.\n\nThe #19996 candidate illustrates the conflict. Its five descriptive artifacts were filed at #20023–#20027; the fold.go behavior/reporting artifact followed at #20028. The independent verdict7495a3ce found no provenance path from those documents to that producer. This is one concrete handoff defect, separate from the verdict's rendering defects; it is not evidence of the author's motive or a measured cost saving. [Case records and probe results](report-order-evidence.json) preserve the distinction.\n\n**Recommendation:** publish in dependency order. Put behavior artifacts first, choosing the intended delivery report last among artifacts that directly answer the same commitment. Then publish descriptive companions on the behavior they actually describe. A companion that does not separately report the commitment need not copy a direct promise edge merely because neighboring artifacts have one. Keep every other meaningful basis; do not remove provenance to manipulate the display. Put the final explicit artifact manifest in the review handoff, where all identifiers already exist.\n\nI tested the simple case against current [completion selection](internal/workroom/fold.go@1fba3d550091f720e48521bb89f222bd4c218af8:3643) and [exact review binding](internal/reviewguard/binding.go@1fba3d550091f720e48521bb89f222bd4c218af8:260). With one behavior report on the promise and a later document resting on that behavior, actual Fold and Resolve preserve and accept the intended primary. Adding a direct promise edge to the document selects it instead, and the guard correctly refuses the old primary. Both controls passed in 0.260s; the [probe](report-order-probe_test.go) is attached. This proves the two-artifact case, not every combined delivery or the full merge path.\n\nBefore review, inspect each actual Commitment.Report and explicitly name the complete examined set. A companion that really reports another commitment must retain that relationship and be included in binding. [Existing gs batch labels](docs/reference/gs/batch.md@1fba3d550091f720e48521bb89f222bd4c218af8:50) can reduce manual ID copying in dependency order; they remain per-act and non-atomic.\n\nThis qualifies #19016 and complements #19969's preparation check. It needs no new state, forced-primary flag, weaker review rule or retrospective history rewrite. Apply it where the dependencies fit; preserve exact-path publication and independent review. Advice for your review, not a new adopted policy.","body":{"head":"1fba3d550091f720e48521bb89f222bd4c218af8","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:21b2806ed4f252e16193552b46606a10ae90eaa7, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:7495a3ce986d35d1ab56647d7fd4b6e22434c02c stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:001b2e3b06105d22946682cb90201bc66c1c6a26, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:00ded1b22f0fb16f85f5770e269c04a8dfb32120, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:01c4e8b02601b3b35241c2baffd15e16e3e3d09d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:03270b167c8f14b55a31919b2489715ee513b20f and 182 more"}}